"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[10340],{610340:e=>{e.exports="# Tableau XML Extraction\n\nReference patterns for finding workbook elements in parsed Tableau XML (`.twb` body). Use XPath when the structure is reliable; fall back to regex when content can span tags or you only need a fast cardinality check.\n\nTag names below are the ones Tableau emits. Treat attribute names as case-sensitive. Both `'` and `\"` appear as attribute quotes — write patterns that accept either when scanning across a workbook you have not seen.\n\n## Quick Recipe\n\nFor broad enumeration, **count first, then narrow**:\n\n1. Run a count-only regex to gauge how many matches exist.\n2. If the count is small (≤ ~50), pull all matches at once.\n3. If the count is large, narrow the pattern (specific name, specific role) before fetching content.\n\nThis avoids dumping multi-MB XML bodies into context.\n\n## Datasources\n\n```\n//datasource\n//datasource[@name=$name]\n//datasource[@caption=$caption]\n```\n\nEach `<datasource>` carries `name` (internal id, often `federated.*` or the original DB name) and `caption` (display name). Inside it: `<connection>`, one or more `<relation>` blocks, `<column>` definitions, and (optionally) `<aliases>` and `<filter>` blocks.\n\n**Connection class** distinguishes live vs extract:\n\n```\n//datasource/connection/@class\n```\n\nClass `hyper` indicates a `.hyper` extract; classes like `snowflake`, `redshift`, `databricks` indicate live connections. Live connections expose the original `[catalog].[schema].[table]` paths in `<relation type='table'>`. Hyper extracts also resolve the original paths in `<relation>` — treat them the same way.\n\n## Tables, Joins, and Relationships\n\nTableau workbooks have two layers above the raw tables. Check the logical layer first (relationships) and only fall back to the physical layer (joins) when no logical model is present.\n\n### Logical layer — Relationships (Tableau 2020.2+)\n\nModern workbooks use a logical model: noodles between **logical tables** (objects) defined by relationship expressions, evaluated lazily at query time. This is the layer you should map onto metric view `joins:` whenever it exists.\n\n```\n//datasource//object-graph\n//datasource//object-graph/objects/object\n//datasource//object-graph/objects/object/@id\n//datasource//object-graph/objects/object/@caption\n//datasource//object-graph/relationships/relationship\n//datasource//object-graph/relationships/relationship/expression\n```\n\nEach `<object>` is a logical table backed by one physical relation in the connection (look for a child reference like `<properties><relation>...</relation></properties>` or an `id` matching a `<relation>` name).\n\nEach `<relationship>` element holds one or more join-key expressions. The expression body uses `[Object].[Field]` references on each side — these are logical-table references, not physical-table references. Resolve each side back to its underlying `<relation type='table'>` via the object's id.\n\nIf you find any `<object-graph>` in a datasource, ignore the physical-layer joins below for that datasource — relationships are the source of truth.\n\n### Physical layer — Tables and Joins\n\nWhen a datasource has no logical model (legacy workbooks, or custom-SQL connections), the physical layer is everything.\n\n**Physical tables (leaves of the relation tree):**\n\n```\n//relation[@type='table']\n//relation[@type='table']/@table\n//relation[@type='table'][@name=$name]\n```\n\n`@table` is `[catalog].[schema].[table]` — strip the brackets and join with dots to get `catalog.schema.table`.\n\n**Joins:**\n\n```\n//relation[@type='join']\n//relation[@type='join']/@join          (* type: inner | left | right | full *)\n//relation[@type='join']/clause         (* the ON expression *)\n```\n\nA `<relation type='join'>` contains its two operand relations as nested children plus a `<clause>` element holding the join condition. The condition uses `[Table].[Column]` references — both sides resolve back to the leaf tables you find with the table query above.\n\n**Joined-table column ownership.** Inside each `<relation>`, columns belonging to that relation appear as `<columns><column .../></columns>` blocks. Use these to build a column → parent-table map; you'll need it when picking the metric view's `source` table and when qualifying ambiguous join references.\n\n## Columns and Fields\n\n**All declared columns (base + calculated):**\n\n```\n//column\n//column[@role='measure']\n//column[@role='dimension']\n//column[@datatype='date']\n//column[@name=$bracketed_name]   (* e.g. '[Sales]' *)\n```\n\nUseful attributes:\n\n- `name` — bracketed identifier, e.g. `[Sales Amount]`. Used for cross-references.\n- `caption` — display label.\n- `role` — `dimension` | `measure`.\n- `datatype` — `string` | `integer` | `real` | `date` | `datetime` | `boolean`.\n- `type` — `nominal` | `ordinal` | `quantitative`.\n- `default-format` — date/number format string, when present.\n\n**Calculated fields** (a `<column>` whose body contains a `<calculation>`):\n\n```\n//column[calculation]\n//column[calculation[@class='tableau']]\n//column[calculation]/@name\n```\n\nThe formula is the `formula` attribute on the inner `<calculation>` and **commonly spans newlines** in Tableau's pretty-printed output. For multi-line formulas, prefer regex with `[\\s\\S]` over XPath:\n\n```\n<column[^>]*name='\\[([^']+)\\]'[^>]*>[\\s\\S]*?<calculation[^>]*formula='([\\s\\S]*?)'[^>]*/>\n```\n\n(Use the case-insensitive flag if the workbook mixes quote styles.)\n\n## LOD and Table Calculations\n\nLOD expressions and table calcs live inside the `formula` attribute, not as separate XML nodes. Identify them by string pattern after extracting the formula:\n\n- LOD: substring matches `\\{(?:FIXED|INCLUDE|EXCLUDE)?\\s.*?:.*?\\}` (DOTALL — formulas may span newlines).\n- Table calc: substring contains any of the table-calc function names listed in `calc_to_sql.md` (e.g. `RUNNING_SUM`, `TOTAL`, `WINDOW_AVG`, `RANK`, `INDEX`).\n\n## Aliases (per-value display overrides)\n\n```\n//column/aliases/alias\n//column[@name=$col]/aliases/alias/@key\n//column[@name=$col]/aliases/alias/@value\n```\n\nEach `<alias key='raw_value' value='Display'>` becomes one `WHEN` arm in the dimension's `CASE` expression. See \"Tableau Value Aliases\" in `calc_to_sql.md`.\n\n## Worksheets, Marks, Shelves\n\n**Worksheets:**\n\n```\n//worksheet\n//worksheet/@name\n//worksheets/worksheet[@name=$sheet]\n```\n\n**Mark class** (chart type) is a style format attribute, not a top-level element:\n\n```\n//worksheet//style-rule[@element='mark']/format[@attr='mark-class']/@value\n```\n\nCommon values: `Automatic`, `Bar`, `Line`, `Area`, `Square`, `Circle`, `Shape`, `Pie`, `Text`, `Map`, `Polygon`, `Gantt Bar`, `Density`, `Box`. When the value is `Automatic`, resolve the actual mark from shelf contents per `visuals.md` \"Mark Type Detection Rules\".\n\n**Shelves** (rows, columns, color, size, label, detail, tooltip, page, filter, level-of-detail):\n\n```\n//worksheet//rows\n//worksheet//cols\n//worksheet//encodings/encoding[@attr='color']\n//worksheet//encodings/encoding[@attr='size']\n//worksheet//encodings/encoding[@attr='text']\n//worksheet//encodings/encoding[@attr='detail']\n//worksheet//encodings/encoding[@attr='tooltip']\n//worksheet//encodings/encoding[@attr='angle']\n```\n\nThe `<rows>` and `<cols>` elements contain space-separated field references (e.g. `[federated.xxx].[Order Date]`). Color / size / text shelves carry the field via the `field` attribute on the `<encoding>` element.\n\n**Shelf field aggregation** appears as a `derivation` attribute on `<column-instance>` (`Sum`, `Avg`, `Count`, `CountD`, `Min`, `Max`, `Median`, `Year-Trunc`, `Month-Trunc`, etc.).\n\n## Filters\n\n**Worksheet-level filters** (dimension filters on a sheet):\n\n```\n//worksheet//filter\n//worksheet//filter/@class            (* categorical | quantitative | relative-date | ... *)\n//worksheet//filter/@column\n//worksheet//filter/groupfilter\n//worksheet//filter/groupfilter/groupfilter\n```\n\nA `<filter>` element holds the filter scope and operator. Inside:\n\n- `<groupfilter function='member'>` with nested `<groupfilter level='[Field]' member='value'/>` for include/exclude lists.\n- `<min>` and `<max>` for range filters.\n- `<period>` for relative-date filters.\n\n**Datasource-level filters** sit directly under `<datasource>`:\n\n```\n//datasource/filter\n```\n\nThese map to the metric view's top-level `filter:` field (see `filters.md`).\n\n**Context filters** are flagged with `context='true'` on the worksheet filter; treat them as worksheet-scoped unless multiple sheets share them, in which case promote to the datasource filter.\n\n## Parameters\n\nParameters are **not migratable** — every reference is documented as a skip. You only need to identify parameters by name so calculations and filters that reference them can be cataloged.\n\n```\n//column[@param-domain-type]                              (* parameter declarations — names only *)\n```\n\nCalculations that reference a parameter use `[Parameters].[Param Name]` in the formula; parameter-driven filters carry the same `[Parameters].` prefix. Search for that bracketed prefix to find every dependency. Per the \"Skip Criteria\" decision tree (see `SKILL.md`), every such calculation and filter is skipped — there is no need to extract member lists, allowed ranges, granularities, or default values.\n\n## Dashboards and Layout\n\n```\n//dashboard\n//dashboard/@name\n//dashboard//zone                      (* layout containers *)\n//dashboard//zone[@type='worksheet']\n//dashboard//zone[@type='filter']\n//dashboard//zone[@type='text']\n```\n\nA `<dashboard>` is composed of nested `<zone>` elements. Each leaf zone references either a `<worksheet>` (by `name`), a filter (`@type='filter'`), or static text. Zones carry `x`, `y`, `w`, `h` attributes in Tableau's coordinate system — use these only as ordering hints when grouping worksheets onto pages; the dashboard sub-agent handles its own grid placement.\n\n## From Extracted Joins to Metric View YAML\n\nOnce you have the relations and joins extracted, you need to fold them into the metric view's `source:` and `joins:` fields.\n\n### Pick the source format\n\n```\nIs the Tableau physical layer simple (single table, no custom SQL)?\n├─ YES → Format A: direct table reference + joins: section\n└─ NO (unions, custom SQL, pivots, complex joins, hyper extracts with pre-shaping)\n   └─ Format B: SQL expression in source: that flattens / denormalizes\n```\n\nIf a 3-part name contains hyphens, spaces, or special characters, backtick-quote the offending segment: `` catalog.schema.`My-Table` ``.\n\nFor hyper extracts (connection `class='hyper'`), the original `[catalog].[schema].[table]` paths are already in the relation tree — treat them like live-connection tables.\n\n### Fact table selection\n\nThe Tableau base table (graph root) is often a dimension table, not the fact table. Pick the source from your column → table assignments instead: group measures by their parent table; the table that owns most aggregated measures is the fact table.\n\nIf measures span multiple tables, generate one MV per fact table (see \"One metric view per Tableau data source\" in the system prompt).\n\n### Building nested joins from flat data\n\nFlat join lists must be nested for metric view YAML. First-level joins attach to `source`; deeper joins nest under their parent.\n\nGiven flat joins:\n\n```\nfact_table → dim_a (fk_a = pk_a)\ndim_a → dim_b (fk_b = pk_b)\nfact_table → dim_c (fk_c = pk_c)\n```\n\nNested YAML (fact_table is root):\n\n```yaml\nsource: catalog.schema.fact_table\njoins:\n  - name: dim_a\n    source: catalog.schema.dim_a\n    'on': source.fk_a = dim_a.pk_a\n    joins:\n      - name: dim_b\n        source: catalog.schema.dim_b\n        'on': dim_a.fk_b = dim_b.pk_b\n  - name: dim_c\n    source: catalog.schema.dim_c\n    'on': source.fk_c = dim_c.pk_c\n```\n\n### ON clause construction\n\n- First-level join (left side is the root): use `source.<col>` on the left, `<join_name>.<col>` on the right.\n  - `fk_a = pk_a` with left=fact_table, right=dim_a → `source.fk_a = dim_a.pk_a`\n- Nested join: use the parent join's name on the left.\n  - `fk_b = pk_b` with left=dim_a, right=dim_b → `dim_a.fk_b = dim_b.pk_b`\n- Always quote the `on` key: `'on':`. YAML 1.1 treats unquoted `on` as boolean `true`.\n\n### Field qualification rules\n\nIn dimensions and measures:\n\n- Source-table columns are unqualified: `o_orderdate`, `quantity`.\n- First-level join columns use `<join_name>.<col>`: `customer.c_name`.\n- Nested join columns use the full path from the first-level ancestor: `customer.nation.n_name` (not `nation.n_name`).\n\n### Datasource-level filters\n\nTableau data-source / extract / context filters map to the metric view's top-level `filter:` field — combine multiple filters of the same scope with `AND`. See `filters.md` for the full filter-scope mapping.\n\n## Reusable Building Blocks\n\n**Find every reference to a field across the workbook** (datasource columns, calculations, shelves, filters):\n\n```\n\\[<bracketed_name>\\]\n```\n\nScan for the bracketed name; matches inside `formula=` attributes catch dependencies that XPath alone misses.\n\n**Multi-line content within an attribute** (calculations, SQL, custom formats):\n\nUse regex with `[\\s\\S]` instead of `.`. Example for capturing a formula and its surrounding column:\n\n```\n<column[^>]*name='\\[Sales\\]'[^>]*>[\\s\\S]*?</column>\n```\n\n**Cardinality probe** before pulling content: run any pattern in count mode first, then either narrow or pull content based on the count.\n"}}]);
